    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0252",
      "sequence": 264,
      "meaning": "Janaka asks whom such a prize failed to please, since no one can even string Śaṅkara’s bow, let alone break it; not one ruler can free even a sesame seed’s breadth of ground from it. Let no proud warrior take offence: he now knows the earth to be without heroes. They should abandon hope and go home, for fate has not written Vaidehī’s marriage. He cannot discard his vow without losing accumulated merit, so what can he do but leave the princess unmarried? Had he known the world lacked warriors, he would never have made a vow that rendered him ridiculous. Hearing Janaka, all the men and women grieve for Jānakī. Lakṣmaṇa bristles; his brows bend, lips quiver and eyes redden with anger. Out of awe for Raghubīra he can barely speak, but he bows his head at Rāma’s lotus feet and utters forceful words.",
