    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0255",
      "sequence": 267,
      "meaning": "The night of the kings’ hopes ends, and their starry array of words no longer shines. Proud rulers, like night-lilies, close up; deceitful ones, like owls, hide. Sages and gods, like cakravāka birds, are freed of grief and rain flowers in service. Rāma lovingly bows at the guru’s feet and asks the sages’ permission. The Lord of the world walks forward naturally with the lovely gait of a noble elephant in rut; every man and woman in the city thrills and overflows with joy as he moves. They invoke ancestors, gods and former merit: if any power belongs to our good deeds, then, O Gaṇeśa, let Rāma break Śiva’s bow as though it were a lotus fibre. Seeing Rāma with love, Sītā’s mother summons the friends nearby and laments under the force of affection.",
